# The ledger of `sql/mysql`: what a program does with a connection to MySQL
# or MariaDB, and what their client/server protocol offers, measured against
# Go's `github.com/go-sql-driver/mysql` v1 (its `go doc -all`, the module
# fetched into the scratch folder on 2026-11-18) and Python's PyMySQL 1.2.4,
# a PEP 249 driver (`dir()` of `pymysql`, its `Connection` and its `Cursor`,
# installed into the scratch folder the same day). Rust's standard library
# has no database, so its line is `-` in every block. What does not depend on
# the database (values, rows, results, failure codes) is the ledger of `sql`;
# what `database/sql` itself offers (a pool, `Stmt`, `Tx`) is stated there.
#
# Every expected value of the tests was observed or written from the
# protocol's documentation: the packets' bytes from MySQL's "Client/Server
# Protocol" pages (HandshakeV10, HandshakeResponse41, SSLRequest, OK, ERR,
# EOF, ColumnDefinition41, the text and binary result sets, COM_STMT_PREPARE,
# COM_STMT_EXECUTE, COM_STMT_CLOSE, COM_SET_OPTION), the two scrambles from
# PyMySQL's `_auth.scramble_native_password` and `_auth.scramble_caching_sha2`.

capability sql-mysql/open  a connection opened and logged in
  heroes  mysql.open, mysql.options, mysql.Options, mysql.DEFAULT_PORT
  go      NewConnector, NewConfig, Config, MySQLDriver, MySQLDriver.Open, MySQLDriver.OpenConnector
  rust    -
  python  connect, Connect, Connection, Connection.connect
  note    one connection, which the program owes a `close`; no pool (the ledger of `sql`)

capability sql-mysql/url  a connection's options read from a URL
  heroes  mysql.parse_url
  go      ParseDSN
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    MySQL Shell's and Connector/J's URI form, `mysql://user:password@host:port/database?ssl-mode=...&ssl-ca=...`, and `mariadb://`; Go's own DSN form, `user:password@tcp(host:port)/database?param=value`, is a block of its own below

capability sql-mysql/go-dsn  options read from Go's own DSN text, `user:password@tcp(host:port)/database`
  heroes  refused: a second spelling of the URL's fields that only Go's driver reads; MySQL's own URI form is read, and `Options` names every field
  go      ParseDSN
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    -

capability sql-mysql/format-url  a connection's options written back as a URL
  heroes  pending
  go      Config.FormatDSN
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `url`'s escapes would write each part; the password would stand in the text

capability sql-mysql/login  a password answered as the server's plugin asks: caching_sha2_password or mysql_native_password
  heroes  mysql.open
  go      Config.AllowNativePasswords, ErrNativePassword, ErrUnknownPlugin
  rust    -
  python  Connection (auth_plugin_map)
  note    the scrambles from `crypto`'s SHA-1 and SHA-256; a switch of plugin (AuthSwitchRequest) followed at most eight times; Go's driver refuses `mysql_native_password` unless `AllowNativePasswords`, where here it is spoken always, MariaDB's default being it

capability sql-mysql/clear-password  the password itself sent where the server asks for it, over TLS alone
  heroes  mysql.open
  go      Config.AllowCleartextPasswords, ErrCleartextPassword
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `caching_sha2_password`'s full exchange, `sha256_password` and `mysql_clear_password` send the password and a zero byte over TLS; without TLS the login fails `unsupported`, where Go's driver sends it in clear when allowed

capability sql-mysql/rsa-password  the password encrypted to the server's RSA key where the connection has no TLS
  heroes  pending
  go      RegisterServerPubKey, DeregisterServerPubKey, Config.ServerPubKey
  rust    -
  python  Connection (server_public_key)
  note    MySQL encrypts by RSA-OAEP with SHA-1 and MGF1 over SHA-1; `crypto/keypairs.encrypt` offers SHA-256's OAEP alone. A login over TLS once fills the server's cache, after which the fast answer serves without TLS

capability sql-mysql/old-password  MySQL 4.0's password scheme
  heroes  refused: the scheme is broken (a hash of 64 bits, recovered in seconds), and MySQL removed it in 5.7.5
  go      Config.AllowOldPasswords, ErrOldPassword
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    -

capability sql-mysql/mariadb-plugins  MariaDB's own plugins, client_ed25519 and parsec
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `client_ed25519` signs with a key whose seed is the password of any length, which no Ed25519 of `crypto/keypairs` takes; `parsec` (MariaDB 11.6) derives a seed by PBKDF2 and signs with Ed25519, which macOS's own cryptography lacks

capability sql-mysql/tls  TLS begun after the greeting, the server's certificate checked for its authority and host
  heroes  mysql.open, mysql.Security, mysql.parse_url
  go      RegisterTLSConfig, DeregisterTLSConfig, Config.TLS, Config.TLSConfig, ErrNoTLS
  rust    -
  python  Connection (ssl, ssl_ca, ssl_verify_cert, ssl_verify_identity, ssl_disabled)
  note    the default, `ssl-mode=VERIFY_IDENTITY`; through `tls.client_over`, so on Linux and Windows. `PREFERRED`, `REQUIRED` and `VERIFY_CA` are refused (below)

capability sql-mysql/tls-macos  TLS to MySQL on macOS
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `tls` begins TLS on macOS only over a connection it makes, after fixed steps; MySQL's TLS begins after the server's greeting, a packet of a length it writes itself. A step of `tls` that reads one packet by its length (three bytes, the lowest first) would carry it

capability sql-mysql/tls-weaker  TLS that falls back to plain text, or does not check the host
  heroes  refused: `PREFERRED` falls back to plain text in silence, `REQUIRED` and `VERIFY_CA` accept whoever presents a certificate; each lets whoever sits between read the connection or pose as the server
  go      Config.AllowFallbackToPlaintext
  rust    -
  python  Connection (ssl_verify_cert=False)
  note    -

capability sql-mysql/tls-client-certificate  a client's own certificate presented to the server
  heroes  pending
  go      Config.TLS
  rust    -
  python  Connection (ssl_cert, ssl_key, ssl_key_password)
  note    `tls.Client` holds no certificate of the client's

capability sql-mysql/close  a connection closed, and asked whether it is
  heroes  mysql.close, mysql.is_closed
  go      ErrInvalidConn
  rust    -
  python  Connection.close, Connection.open
  note    `COM_QUIT`, then the socket closed

capability sql-mysql/ping  a connection asked whether it still works
  heroes  mysql.ping
  go      Config.CheckConnLiveness
  rust    -
  python  Connection.ping
  note    `COM_PING`; PyMySQL's `ping(reconnect=True)` reconnects, which here is `open` again

capability sql-mysql/exec  a statement that changes rows run with its values bound, its count and id read
  heroes  mysql.exec
  go      Result, Result.AllRowsAffected, Result.AllLastInsertIds
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.execute, Connection.affected_rows, Connection.insert_id
  note    with values, prepared, run and closed (`COM_STMT_PREPARE`, `COM_STMT_EXECUTE`, `COM_STMT_CLOSE`); without, `COM_QUERY`. `last_id` fails `not_found` where the statement made no `AUTO_INCREMENT` value, where Go and Python answer 0

capability sql-mysql/query  a statement's rows read into values by their column's type
  heroes  mysql.query, mysql.query_one
  go      NullTime, NullTime.Scan, NullTime.Value
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.execute, Cursor.fetchone, Cursor.fetchall, Cursor.fetchmany, Connection.query
  note    every row read before it answers; the same value from the text and the binary protocols, a `FLOAT` rounded to its single in both (Go and Python read the text's decimal as a double)

capability sql-mysql/stream-rows  rows read one at a time as the server sends them, never all held
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.scroll
  note    PyMySQL's `SSCursor`; `LIMIT` and `OFFSET` read a page meanwhile

capability sql-mysql/several-results  every result of a statement that answers several, as `CALL` does
  heroes  pending
  go      Result.AllRowsAffected, Result.AllLastInsertIds
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.nextset, Connection.next_result
  note    every result is read, so the connection stays in step; `query` answers the first that has columns and `exec` the counts of the last

capability sql-mysql/exec-script  several statements separated by semicolons run as one text
  heroes  mysql.exec_script
  go      Config.MultiStatements, ErrPktSyncMul
  rust    -
  python  Connection (client_flag=MULTI_STATEMENTS)
  note    allowed for this call alone by `COM_SET_OPTION`, so `exec` and `query` never run a second statement smuggled into the first

capability sql-mysql/prepare  a statement prepared once and run many times
  heroes  mysql.prepare, mysql.exec_prepared, mysql.query_prepared, mysql.close_statement, mysql.Statement
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Cursor.executemany
  note    `database/sql`'s `Stmt` is the ledger of `sql`'s; PyMySQL prepares nothing and interpolates every value

